#
# Declares that the FM antenna is built in.
#
# This phone has no headphone jack, which on other devices doubles as the
# antenna. The LineageOS FMRadio app allows for that: in FmService.isAntennaAvailable()
#
#     if (FmUtils.hasBuiltInFmAntennaSupport()) return true;
#     return mAudioManager.isWiredHeadsetOn();
#
# and hasBuiltInFmAntennaSupport() reads the property set below. Without it the
# app just keeps asking for headphones to be plugged in, which cannot be
# plugged in.
#
# It cannot go in build.prop: ro.vendor.* properties declared in system files
# are rejected by init, and the vendor image is the stock one, which we do not
# rebuild.
on early-init
    setprop ro.vendor.builtin_fm_antenna_support 1

# Gives the radio app access to the tuner.
#
# The kernel creates /dev/fm as media:media, but the FMRadio app runs as system
# and is left out:
#
#     FMLIB_COM: Open /dev/fm failed, Permission denied
#     FMLIB_CORE: FMR_pwr_up failed, [ret=-1]
#
# and all the user sees is "Currently unavailable", with no clue why. In the
# stock firmware the app lived in product and ran with different privileges;
# here we change the ownership of the node instead.
#
# The trigger cannot be "on boot": at that point /dev/fm does not exist yet,
# because it is created by the fmradio_drv.ko module, which the vendor loads
# only once the connectivity chip is ready (vendor.connsys.driver.ready=yes).
# The chown would fail silently. Once boot is complete the node is certainly there.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    chown system system /dev/fm
    chmod 0660 /dev/fm
